  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <div class="faq">
    <h3>How should a restaurant respond to a bad review?</h3>
    <p>Address the reviewer by name, acknowledge the specific problem, apologise once, say what changed and give a direct phone number or email for the details. Google&#39;s own reply tips say the same and add: keep it short and simple. Do not argue in public.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>How long should a restaurant review response be?</h3>
    <p>Google&#39;s advice is to keep it short and simple. Three to five sentences, roughly 50 to 100 words, is enough for a name, the specific point, an apology where it is due, what you changed and a contact.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Do restaurant review responses help Google ranking?</h3>
    <p>Google has not said that replies are a ranking factor. It says more reviews and positive ratings can help local ranking, and that replies show you value feedback. The evidence is in <a href="/blog/does-responding-to-reviews-help-seo">does responding to reviews help SEO?</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Should a restaurant reply to every review?</h3>
    <p>Google says: instead of sending the same thank-you to everyone, focus on reviews where you can share a helpful update or answer a question. Reply to every negative or mixed review and to praise that names a dish or a person; a short, varied thank-you is enough for the rest.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq" style="border:none">
    <h3>What if a guest demands a free meal or they will post a bad review?</h3>
    <p>Do not pay or negotiate. Google calls extortion scams a serious policy violation and says not to engage with or pay the people behind them. Save screenshots of every demand and report it through Google&#39;s extortion report form.</p>
  </div>

